                "text": "Let me understand the rule from the verifier.\n\nThe verifier:\n- x0 = palette(I) \u2014 set of colors in input\n- x1 = contained(ONE, x0) \u2014 blue present?\n- x2 = contained(TWO, x0) \u2014 red present?\n- x3 = branch(x1, UNITY, TWO_BY_TWO) \u2014 if blue present, center=(1,1), else (2,2)\n- x4 = branch(x2, RIGHT, x3) \u2014 if red present, (0,1), else x3\n- x5 = fork(combine, vfrontier, hfrontier) \u2014 union of vertical and horizontal frontier through x4\n- x6 = x5(x4)\n- x7 = canvas(ZERO, (3,3)) \u2014 3x3 black\n- x8 = fill(x7, FIVE, x6) \u2014 fill 5 (grey) at those indices within bounds (fill clips to 0<=i<3, 0<=j<3)\n\nLet's compute for each case:\n- Blue present (and not red): x3=(1,1), x4=(1,1). vfrontier(1,1) = column 1 all rows, hfrontier(1,1) = row 1 all columns. Intersection within 3x3: plus sign. Output all 5 except corners 0. Matches example (input has 1s, blue): output plus. Yes.\n- Red present: x4=(0,1). vfrontier = column 1, hfrontier = row 0. Fill within 3x3: column 1 (rows 0,1,2) and row 0 (cols 0,1,2). So cells: (0,0),(0,1),(0,2),(1,1),(2,1). That's black at... 5-filled cells: row0 all three, row1 col1, row2 col1. Black cells: (1,0),(1,2),(2,0),(2,2)? Wait row2: only col1 filled, so (2,0),(2,2) black. Hmm the rule says \"black on the lower left, middle left, middle right and lower right (making a grey T shape)\". Grey T upside down? Grey cells: top row full, middle col. Black: (1,0),(1,2),(2,0),(2,2). Rule description says black on lower left, middle left, middle right, lower right. Actually black cells: (1,0) middle left, (1,2) middle right, (2,0) lower left, (2,2) lower right. Yes matches! The grey is a T (top row + middle column going down).\n- Green present? Wait, green is color 3. The verifier doesn't reference THREE! Hmm. Natural language mentions green shapes -> 2x2 black square upper left. But verifier only checks ONE and TWO. If neither blue nor red present, x1=False -> x3=TWO_BY_TWO=(2,2), x4=(2,2). vfrontier(2,2)=column 2, hfrontier(2,2)=row 2. Fill: (0,2),(1,2),(2,0),(2,1),(2,2). Grey cells: right column full and bottom row full. Black cells: (0,0),(0,1),(1,0),(1,1). That's 2x2 black in upper left! So \"green\" in natural language is actually just the else case \u2014 any input without blue/red gives 2x2 black upper-left.\n\nSo the rule: examine palette. If blue (1) present -> plus. If red (2) present -> T. Else -> 2x2 black upper left.\n\nNote the order: x1 checks blue first, then x2 overrides with red. So if both blue and red present, red wins (RIGHT overrides). Let's check: x3 = branch(x1, UNITY, TWO_BY_TWO); x4 = branch(x2, RIGHT, x3). So if red present, x4=RIGHT regardless of blue. So red takes priority.\n\nIf blue present and red absent -> plus.\nIf red present (with or without blue) -> T (grey T, top row + middle column).\nElse -> 2x2 black upper-left (grey: right column + bottom row).\n\nNow I need to create 7 demonstration pairs. They should cover:\n- blue only -> plus\n- red only -> T\n- green only / neither -> 2x2 black upper left\n- red+blue -> T\n- other colors only -> 2x2 black.\n\nI should make diverse inputs. Let me create various grids.\n\nLet me generate inputs:\n\n1. Blue shapes only (1s and 0s), e.g., 5x5 random with 1s. Output plus.\n2. Red shapes only (2s and 0s). Output T.\n3. Green shapes (3s) -> output 2x2 black upper left (grey right col + bottom row).\n4. Blue + red mixed -> T.\n5. Other color like 4 (yellow) -> 2x2 black.\n6. Blue again different shape.\n7. Red again different shape, or green.\n\nI must make sure verifier can evaluate: it just needs a rectangular grid. Palette works.\n\nLet me just call show_transformed_input multiple times with crafted grids. Need \"assemble exactly 7 demonstration pairs\". So call 7 times.\n\nLet me craft:\n\nPair 1 (blue): \n```\n[[0,1,0,0,0],\n [1,0,1,0,0],\n [0,0,0,0,1],\n [0,1,0,1,0],\n [0,0,0,0,0]]\n```\nPalette contains 1 -> plus.\n\nPair 2 (red):\n```\n[[2,0,2,0,0],\n [0,2,0,0,2],\n [0,0,0,2,0],\n [2,0,0,0,0],\n [0,0,2,0,0]]\n```\nOutput T.\n\nPair 3 (green only):\n```\n[[3,0,0,3,0],\n [0,0,3,0,0],\n [0,3,0,0,0],\n [0,0,0,0,3],\n [3,0,0,0,0]]\n```\nPalette no 1, no 2 -> else -> 2x2 black upper-left.\n\nPair 4 (blue + red):\n```\n[[1,0,2,0,0],\n [0,2,0,1,0],\n [0,0,1,0,2],\n [2,0,0,0,0],\n [0,1,0,0,0]]\n```\nRed present -> T.\n\nPair 5 (other color, e.g., 4 yellow):\n```\n[[4,0,0,0,4],\n [0,4,0,4,0],\n [0,0,4,0,0],\n [0,4,0,4,0],\n [4,0,0,0,4]]\n```\n-> 2x2 black upper-left.\n\nPair 6 (blue only, different size, e.g., 3x3):\n```\n[[0,1,0],\n [1,0,1],\n [0,1,0]]\n```\n-> plus.\n\nPair 7 (red only, small):\n```\n[[2,0],\n [0,2]]\n```\n-> T.\n\nHmm wait, could a 1x1? Fine. Let me vary sizes a bit.\n\nActually let me double check the \"green\" case output. x4=(2,2), vfrontier(2,2) = {(i,2) for i in 0..29}, hfrontier = {(2,j)}. Within 3x3: column 2 cells (0,2),(1,2),(2,2), row 2 cells (2,0),(2,1),(2,2). So filled 5 at: (0,2),(1,2),(2,0),(2,1),(2,2). Output:\nrow0: [0,0,5]\nrow1: [0,0,5]\nrow2: [5,5,5]\nBlack upper-left 2x2: (0,0),(0,1),(1,0),(1,1). Yes.\n\nGood. Let me submit these 7.\n\nActually the natural language says \"green shapes\" but verifier ignores green and uses else. Including a green example is good, plus an other-color example to show it's not about green specifically. But careful: if I include both green and other-color, the student might infer \"anything not blue/red\". That's correct = else. Good.\n\nLet me make calls. I'll do them in parallel since independent. Actually each call is independent, can batch. Let me batch all 7 in one block.\n\nWait\u2014the instruction says use show_transformed_input to add a pair; environment computes output. So I call it 7 times. Let me do it.",
